  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "Which of the following statements must be true for any integers $m$ and $n?$\n\nI. $5∣(5m+10n)$\n\nII. $3∣(5m+10n)$\n\nIII. $2∣(5m+10n)$",
  "worked_solution": "The notation $a∣b$ means $a$ divides $b.$\n\nAccording to the definition of integer divisibility, the fact that $a$ divides $b$ means that there exists an integer $k$ such that \n\n$$\nb=ak.\n$$\n\n \nWith that in mind, let's examine our statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that \n\n$$\n5m+10n=5(m+2n).\n$$\n\nSince $x$ and $y$ are integers, $m+2n$ must be an integer, too. So, $5∣(5m+10n).$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, if $m=1$ and $n=0,$ we have $5(1)+10(0)=5,$ which is not divisible by $3.$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, if $m=1$ and $n=0,$ we have $5(1)+10(0)=5,$ which is not divisible by $2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
